  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 AM MDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nI have stated a few times before how much I hate forecasting the \nmonsoon. The models do not handle it well, especially in weakly \nforced situations. An example is tonight where continue to have some \nshowers and embedded thunder in western Wyoming and stretching into \nsouthern Idaho. No guidance had this last night, and nighttime is \nwhere it really struggles. In this case, as has been the case\nmuch of the previous several weeks, having as much vision of it\nas Helen Keller or Stevie Wonder. So, although my confidence in\nthe trend of the coverage of the monsoon is fairly high, \nplacement and timing of any convection, especially at night, is \nvery low.\n\nThe general trend of the showers is down, but some will linger all \nnight. Most guidance does show drier is moving into the area, \nespecially across eastern and southern Wyoming. Precipitable water \nvalues drop another 20 percent or so, and dew points should average \n5 to 10 degrees less than yesterday. This means a lesser coverage of \nshowers and storms (generally no more than a 1 in 3 chance at any \npoint, and less then 1 in 5 at most places), and also less of a\nchance of heavy rain that could lead to local flooding. For \nnow, we confined most POPs to the northwestern half of the \nstate, and I have higher confidence during the day since there \nhas been better model performance during this time frame. And, \nwith more sunshine, temperatures should be somewhat warmer. As \nfor tonight, most convection should end after sunset. However, \nmy confidence in dry overnight period is low given the terrible \nperformance of the models over the past few weeks with the \nmonsoon during the nighttime hours. I just don't have the \nconfidence to pinpoint an area at this time.\n\nMonday at this point looks fairly similar to Sunday, with mainly \nisolated convection and largely across northern and western Wyoming \nwith less activity to the south and east. Temperatures should run \nfairly similar to Sunday. Some guidance is giving a surge of \nmoisture at night and possible increase in activity but guidance is \nsplit. This, plus the models weakness in nighttime monsoon \nconvection, confidence is low. With precipitable waters around 125 \nto 150 percent of normal on Tuesday, this also looks like a \nmore active day with temperatures close to seasonal averages.\n\nThere has been a bit of a change for Wednesday, and there is decent \nmodel agreement on this. It is that most show drier air moving in \nfrom the southwest and lessening the chance of showers and \nthunderstorms, with the best chance across eastern Wyoming. The \ndrier pattern looks to hold at least into Friday with near normal \nprecipitable water across the area and high pressure building \nnorthward out of the desert southwest. The forecast for Friday \nis probably the wettest it would be. Monsoonal moisture may \nincrease again for next weekend, but timing this far out is \nuncertain. And with the ridge building northward again, \ntemperatures will be on the rise again. Highs will begin to \nrise again on Thursday. By Friday and into next weekend, highs \nin the 90s look fairly commonplace East of the Divide and in the\n80s West of the Divide.",
